const P5_REAS = {
 "questions": [
  { "id":"p5r_01","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"How many different sources of the code of the pattern are possible? 521478, 721478, 521498, 721498","opts":["4", "3", "9", "6"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Comparing the four codes digit by digit (5-2-1-4-7-8, 7-2-1-4-7-8, 5-2-1-4-9-8, 7-2-1-4-9-8), only two positions ever change: the 1st digit (5 or 7) and the 5th digit (7 or 9), while the rest stay fixed at 2-1-4-_-8. With 2 choices for each of the 2 varying positions, exactly 2x2=4 different codes are possible — matching the 4 codes already shown.",
    "trick":"When a question shows several similar-looking codes and asks how many are 'possible', line the digits up in columns first — count exactly which positions actually vary and how many options each has, then multiply those counts together." },
  { "id":"p5r_02","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"What word is formed by (0,4)(4,3)(2,1)(3,4)? 0 1 2 3 4 0 J B D C L 1 F I A J H 2 D A K L E 3 B K G I A 4 K C E L L","opts":["LKAA", "LLLA", "LLKA", "LLAA"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"Using the grid (rows/columns 0-4): (0,4)=row0,col4=L; (4,3)=row4,col3=L; (2,1)=row2,col1=A; (3,4)=row3,col4=A. Reading them in order gives L, L, A, A = LLAA.",
    "trick":"For grid/matrix-coordinate questions, always read coordinates as (row, column) consistently and look up the exact cell at that row-column intersection — write out the grid on paper first if it's not already laid out clearly." },
  { "id":"p5r_03","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"If MONDAY is coded as 1315144125 , how is SUNDAY coded?","opts":["1921124125", "1821124126", "1921144125", "1921144127"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"MONDAY's code (1315144125) is simply each letter's alphabet position written one after another: M=13, O=15, N=14, D=4, A=1, Y=25, concatenated as '13'+'15'+'14'+'4'+'1'+'25'. Applying the same method to SUNDAY: S=19, U=21, N=14, D=4, A=1, Y=25, giving '19'+'21'+'14'+'4'+'1'+'25' = 1921144125.",
    "trick":"When a word's code is a long string of digits with no spaces, check if it's simply each letter's alphabet position written back-to-back (not padded to 2 digits) — single-digit positions like D=4 or A=1 appear as just one digit, which is what makes the total length match." },
  { "id":"p5r_04","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Some conditional data is provided to you in form of statement you have to check which of the following statement are logically inference from given statement. Statement: The store manager noticed that sales of umbrellas increased by 300% over the past three days, while sales of sunscreen and beach items dropped significantly. What can you reasonably infer?","opts":["The store is having a special promotion on umbrellas", "There has been unexpected rainy weather", "Beach season is ending", "Customers prefer umbrellas to beach items"],"ans":1,
    "exp":"Umbrella sales rising sharply while sunscreen and beach-item sales drop points most directly to a shift in weather — unexpected rain would explain BOTH trends simultaneously (more need for umbrellas, less interest in beach activities) without requiring any additional assumption about promotions or seasonal timing.",
    "trick":"When multiple seemingly separate trends (umbrella sales up, beach-item sales down) can be explained by ONE simple common cause (weather), that shared explanation is a stronger inference than one that only accounts for a single trend (like a promotion, which wouldn't explain the beach-item drop)." },
  { "id":"p5r_05","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"What should come in place of the question mark (?) in the given series? 20,21,25,34,50,75,\" ? \"","opts":["96", "121", "111", "100"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"The differences between consecutive terms are perfect squares: 21-20=1(1^2), 25-21=4(2^2), 34-25=9(3^2), 50-34=16(4^2), 75-50=25(5^2). The next difference should be 6^2=36, giving 75+36=111.",
    "trick":"When a series' differences look like 1, 4, 9, 16, 25..., recognize them immediately as perfect squares (1^2, 2^2, 3^2...) — the next difference is simply the next integer squared." },
  { "id":"p5r_06","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Identify the odd group: 18, 24, 30, 35","opts":["35", "24", "30", "18"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"18, 24, and 30 are all exactly divisible by 6, while 35 is not (35/6 is not a whole number) — making 35 the odd one out.",
    "trick":"For a short 'odd one out' number set, quickly test divisibility by small numbers (2, 3, 5, 6) shared by most of the group — the one number that breaks the shared divisibility stands out immediately." },
  { "id":"p5r_07","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"A, B, C, D, E, F, G and H are sitting around a square table facing the centre of the table. Some of them are sitting at the corners, while some are sitting at the exact centre of the sides. Only three people sit between C and D. Both C and D sit at corners of the table. A sits second to the right of D. B sits to the immediate right of F. B is not an immediate neighbour of D. G is not an immediate neighbour of A. Only one person sits between G and H. Who sits second to the right of E?","opts":["F", "C", "H", "D"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"With C and D both at corners and exactly 3 people seated between them going one way around (meaning they sit directly opposite each other on the square table), and working through the remaining clues (A second right of D, B immediate right of F, G not neighbouring A, G and H one seat apart), the seating works out to A-E-C-F-B-G-D-H in clockwise order. Two seats to the right of E lands on F.",
    "trick":"'Only N people between X and Y' on an 8-seat square table (4 corners + 4 mid-sides) translates directly into a fixed seat-gap — work this out as a number of seats first (3 people between = 4 seats apart), then treat the rest of the puzzle just like a standard circular arrangement." },
  { "id":"p5r_08","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Eight symbol-number clusters are given below, three of which are exactly the same. (A) 74*&4%48@23*7^+#  (B) 74*&4%43@23*7$+#  (C) 74*&4%43@23*7^+#  (D) 74*&4%43@23*7^+#  (E) 74*&4$43@23*7^+#  (F) 74*&4%43@33*7^+#  (G) 74*&4&43@23*7^+#  (H) 74*&4%43@23*7^+#  Find the three clusters that are exactly the same.","opts":["A, C and E", "B, C and E", "C, D and H", "C, F and H"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"Ignoring the stray extra spaces (a typesetting artifact, not a real difference), clusters C, D, and H all read identically as '74*&4%43@23*7^+#'. Cluster A has '48' instead of '43', B has '$' instead of '^', E has '$' instead of '%', F has '33' instead of '23', and G has '&' instead of '%' — each a genuine one-character difference.",
    "trick":"When comparing long symbol strings, mentally split them into short chunks and compare chunk-by-chunk across all options at once — spotting one swapped character is far easier in short segments than scanning the whole string left to right." },
  { "id":"p5r_09","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "svg":"<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\">",
    "q":"In each question below is given a statement followed by two assumptions numbered I and II. You have to consider the statement and the following assumptions and decide which of the assumptions is implicit in the statement. Statement : Traffic jams on most of the roads in the city have become a regular feature during monsoon. Assumption : I. Material used for road construction cannot withstand the fury of monsoon resulting into innumerable pot holes on the roads.   II. Number of vehicles coming on the road is much more in monsoon as compared to other seasons.","opts":["If only assumption I is implicit", "If only assumption II is implicit", "If both I and II are implicit.", "If neither I nor II is implicit"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"Traffic jams being 'a regular feature during monsoon' could stem from many possible causes (waterlogging, reduced visibility, accidents) — neither assumption I (poor road material specifically) nor assumption II (more vehicles specifically) is a NECESSARY condition for the statement to be true; both introduce a specific unstated cause that the general observation doesn't require.",
    "trick":"An assumption is only 'implicit' if the statement COULD NOT be true without it — when a general observation (jams happen in monsoon) could easily be explained by several different specific causes, none of those specific causes is a necessary implicit assumption." },
  { "id":"p5r_10","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"If for any numbers a and b, a @b is defined as 4a + 3b² + 1, then what is the value of 10 @ 10?","opts":["335", "337", "341", "332"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"Substituting a=10, b=10 into 4a+3b^2+1: 4(10)+3(100)+1 = 40+300+1 = 341.",
    "trick":"For custom-operator questions ('a@b is defined as...'), substitute the given values directly into the formula exactly as written, handling the squared term (3b^2, not (3b)^2) carefully before adding everything up." },
  { "id":"p5r_11","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"In this question, the statement is followed by two conclusions. Find out which conclusion is true based on the given statement. Statement: L ≤ U > G > S = M Conclusions: I. L < G II. M < U","opts":["Neither I nor II is true", "Only II is true", "Only I is true", "Both I and II are true"],"ans":1,
    "exp":"From G>S=M and U>G, we get U>G>M by transitivity, directly confirming Conclusion II (M<U). But Conclusion I (L<G) can't be confirmed: L≤U only places L at or below U, while U>G is a separate fact about U and G — with no direct link forcing L below G specifically, L could still be greater than, equal to, or less than G.",
    "trick":"When two different terms (L and G here) are each only connected to a common THIRD term (U) rather than to each other directly, resist the temptation to assume a relationship between them — trace the actual chain of inequalities link by link." },
  { "id":"p5r_12","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"In the following question, select the number which can be placed at the sign of question mark (?) from the given alternatives. 6 5 ? 180 1 7 3 42 3 8 2 96","opts":["1", "2", "3", "6"],"ans":2,
    "exp":"Each row follows the pattern: (1st number) x (2nd number) x (3rd number) x 2 = 4th number. Checking: 1x7x3x2=42 (matches), 3x8x2x2=96 (matches). Applying to the first row: 6x5x?x2=180, so ?=180/60=3.",
    "trick":"When three numbers map to a fourth, test simple combinations (product, sum) against the ROWS you're already given FIRST — once a consistent rule like 'product x 2' checks out on two complete rows, apply it confidently to the incomplete one." },
  { "id":"p5r_13","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Select the option which is related to the third word in the same way as the second word is related to the first word. NOPQ : MNOP :: HIJK : ?","opts":["GHIJ", "GHJJ", "HHIJ", "GIIJ"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"NOPQ to MNOP shifts every letter back by 1 (N-1=M, O-1=N, P-1=O, Q-1=P). Applying the same -1 shift to HIJK: H-1=G, I-1=H, J-1=I, K-1=J, giving GHIJ.",
    "trick":"Verify a suspected letter-shift by checking it holds across ALL FOUR letters of the given example before applying it to the new word — a shift that works on just one or two letters by coincidence isn't the actual rule." },
  { "id":"p5r_14","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "svg":"<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\">",
    "q":"How many squares are there in the following figure?","opts":["60", "42", "37", "30"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"Count squares by size: in a 4x4 grid, there are 16 individual 1x1 squares, 9 squares of size 2x2, 4 squares of size 3x3, and 1 square of size 4x4 (the whole grid). Total = 16+9+4+1 = 30.",
    "trick":"For an n x n grid, the number of k x k squares is always (n-k+1)^2 — sum this formula for every size from 1x1 up to the full grid size instead of trying to count squares by eye, which easily misses the larger overlapping ones." },
  { "id":"p5r_15","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"If 'P' means '÷', 'Q' means '× ', 'R' means '+ ' and 'S' means '-', then 16 Q 12 P 6 R 2 S 4 =?","opts":["30", "38", "32", "42"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Substituting the real signs (Q becomes x, P becomes /, R becomes +, S becomes -): 16x12/6+2-4. Following BODMAS: 16x12=192, /6=32, then 32+2-4=30.",
    "trick":"After substituting symbols, always apply multiplication/division (left to right) BEFORE addition/subtraction — rushing straight through left to right without respecting BODMAS order is what produces the wrong answer choices here." },
  { "id":"p5r_16","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "svg":"<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\">",
    "q":"Three statements are given, followed by four conclusions numbered I to IV. Assuming the statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance with commonly known facts, decide which of the conclusions logically follow(s) from the given statements.   Statements: All copies are books. Some books are magazines. All magazines are newspapers. Conclusions: I. Some magazines are copies. II. Some magazines are books. III. Some newspapers are magazines. IV. No magazine is copy.","opts":["Only conclusion I and III follow.", "Either conclusion I or IV follows.", "Conclusion II and either I or IV follows.", "Conclusion II and III and either I or IV follows."],"ans":3,
    "exp":"From 'Some books are magazines', the valid converse 'Some magazines are books' (Conclusion II) always follows automatically. Since magazines exist (some books ARE magazines) and 'All magazines are newspapers', it also follows that 'Some newspapers are magazines' (Conclusion III). Conclusions I ('Some magazines are copies') and IV ('No magazine is copy') are direct opposites of each other — since neither can be individually proven true or false with certainty, but one of this contradictory pair MUST be true, SSC convention accepts 'either I or IV' as following. Overall: II and III follow, plus either I or IV.",
    "trick":"When two conclusions are exact opposites of each other (one says 'some X are Y', the other says 'no X is Y') and neither is individually provable, that's a classic 'complementary pair' — the correct answer accepts that one of the two MUST be true, framed as 'either...or', even though you can't say which one." },
  { "id":"p5r_17","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Each of the digits in the number 8139245 is arranged in ascending order from left to right. Then, the position of how many digits will remain unchanged as compared to that in the original number?","opts":["One", "None", "Two", "Three"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Original: 8-1-3-9-2-4-5. Sorted ascending: 1-2-3-4-5-8-9. Comparing position by position, only the 3rd digit matches in both (3 in both original and sorted) — all other positions change. So exactly ONE digit's position remains unchanged.",
    "trick":"Write the original number and its sorted version directly underneath each other, digit beneath digit, then simply count straight-down matches — don't try to track digit positions mentally." },
  { "id":"p5r_18","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Four numbers are given below, in which three of them follow the same logic, find the different number and mark it as an answer.","opts":["112", "124", "132", "216"],"ans":1,
    "exp":"Multiply the digits of each number: 112 -> 1x1x2=2, 132 -> 1x3x2=6, 216 -> 2x1x6=12. These products (2, 6, 12) are consecutive 'n(n+1)' values: 1x2=2, 2x3=6, 3x4=12. But 124 -> 1x2x4=8, which is NOT of the form n(n+1) (that would need 8=n(n+1), and no whole number n satisfies it) — so 124 breaks the shared pattern.",
    "trick":"When digit-sum/divisibility checks fail to separate an odd-one-out number set, try the PRODUCT of each number's digits instead — here three products form the neat sequence 2, 6, 12 (=1x2, 2x3, 3x4) while the fourth doesn't fit that n(n+1) pattern." },
  { "id":"p5r_19","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Select the option exactly similar to: 45 East 75th St, Unit 3B, New York, NY 10021-3401","opts":["45 E 75th St, Unit 3B, New York, NY 10021-3401", "5 East 75th St, Unit 3B, New York, NY 10021-3401", "45 East 75th Street, Unit 3B, New York, NY 10021-3401", "45 East 75th St, Unit 3B, New York, NY 10021-3401"],"ans":3,
    "exp":"Comparing field-by-field against the reference address: option (a) abbreviates 'East' to 'E'. Option (b) drops the '4' from '45' (reading just '5'). Option (c) expands 'St' to 'Street'. Option (d) matches the reference exactly.",
    "trick":"Watch for single-character drops in house/unit numbers ('45' becoming '5') — these are easy to miss since the rest of a long address looks identical, but a single dropped digit makes it a completely different address." },
  { "id":"p5r_20","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "svg":"<img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:6px auto\">",
    "q":"In a certain code language, A + B means ‘A is the mother of B’, A – B means ‘A is the brother of B’, A @ B means ‘A is the wife of B’, A % B means ‘A is the father of B’. Based on the above, how is B related to H if ‘B + U – N @ C % H’?","opts":["Sister", "Mother's mother", "Mother", "Mother's sister"],"ans":1,
    "exp":"Decode right to left: 'C%H' means C is H's father. 'N@C' means N is C's wife, making N the mother of H (since C is H's father and N is C's wife). 'U-N' means U is N's brother. 'B+U' means B is U's mother — since U and N are siblings (U is N's brother), B is also N's mother. So B is N's mother, and N is H's mother, making B H's maternal grandmother ('Mother's mother').",
    "trick":"In multi-step family-coding chains, decode from the END closest to the known person (H here) working backward, establishing each new person's generation and gender one relation at a time." },
  { "id":"p5r_21","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"Five plays T1, T2, T3, T4 and T5 are to be stage d from Monday to Friday of a week. On each day, only one play will be staged. T4 and T5 should not be the first or last to be staged. T5 should be staged immediately be fo re T3. T2 should be staged immediately after T4. One play is staged betwe en T1 and T2. How many plays are staged after T4?","opts":["3", "0", "2", "1"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Working through the clues (T4 and T5 can't be first or last, T5 immediately before T3, T2 immediately after T4, and exactly one play between T1 and T2) gives the unique schedule: T1, T4, T2, T5, T3 (Monday to Friday). Counting plays staged after T4 (which is on day 2): T2, T5, and T3 — 3 plays.",
    "trick":"For day-by-day scheduling puzzles with 'immediately before/after' clues, treat those as fixed BLOCKS (T4-T2 glued together, T5-T3 glued together) and slot the blocks into the available days — this is faster than testing each play's position independently." },
  { "id":"p5r_22","topic":"Reasoning","qtype":"Previous Paper","lvl":3,"expSec":80,
    "q":"In each question below is given a statement followed by two assumptions numbered I and II. You have to consider the statement and the following assumptions and decide which of the assumptions is implicit in the statement. Statement: The city bus transport corporations has decided to change the routes of three buses plying between points A and B in the city to make them economically viable. Assumptions: I. These buses may get more passengers on the revised routes. II. Many people residing on the old routes may not avail bus services.","opts":["If only assumption I is implicit", "If only assumption II is implicit", "If both I and II are implicit.", "If neither I nor II is implicit"],"ans":0,
    "exp":"Changing bus routes specifically 'to make them economically viable' directly assumes the revised routes will attract more passengers (assumption I) — without this expectation, the decision wouldn't make business sense. Assumption II (people on old routes might stop using the service) describes a RISK the decision might face, not something the decision-makers are assuming to be true when making their choice.",
    "trick":"Distinguish an assumption the decision RELIES ON (more passengers on new routes, needed for viability) from a risk or side-effect the decision might cause (losing old-route riders) — only the former counts as implicit in the decision itself." },
 ]
};